# Proper assertNotNull Usage

## Context
- When validating non-null values in TypeScript code
- When accessing potentially null class properties
- When working with method return values that could be null

## Requirements
- Capture assertNotNull results in descriptive local variables
- NEVER call assertNotNull multiple times on the same object
- Use the returned non-null value throughout the function scope
- Provide specific error messages that include context
- Use assertNotNull early in functions to validate preconditions

## Examples

<example>
```typescript
// CORRECT: Capture result in a local variable
function processGameState(game: Game): void {
  // Assert once and store in a descriptive variable
  const player = assertNotNull(game.player, "Player must be initialized");
  
  // Use the non-null player variable throughout the function
  player.updatePosition();
  player.checkCollisions();
  player.render();
}

// CORRECT: Multiple assertions stored in well-named variables
function initializeGameSystems(scene: Scene): void {
  const worldSystem = assertNotNull(scene.worldSystem, "World system required");
  const inputSystem = assertNotNull(scene.inputSystem, "Input system required");
  const renderSystem = assertNotNull(scene.renderSystem, "Render system required");
  
  worldSystem.generateMap();
  inputSystem.bindControls();
  renderSystem.initialize();
}
```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
```typescript
// INCORRECT: Redundant assertions on the same object
function updatePlayerState(game: Game): void {
  assertNotNull(game.player, "Player required").updatePosition();
  assertNotNull(game.player, "Player required").applyPhysics();
  assertNotNull(game.player, "Player required").render();
}

// INCORRECT: Reassigning class property
class GameManager {
  private player: Player | null = null;
  
  init(): void {
    this.player = new Player();
    // Bad: Redundant assertion and reassignment
    this.player = assertNotNull(this.player, "Player init failed");
  }
}
```
</example>

